   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter"> <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_NICE_01">Hotel Suisse</name>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_NICE">Nice</name>
                            </addrLine>
                        </address>
                    </ab>
                </div> <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>Dear Miss Weaver: <rs type="person" key="joyc00">We</rs> are staying <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">here</seg> for the moment. Perhaps I shall have <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">some news</seg> tomorrow from <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI">Paris</name> or <name type="geo" key="G_GBR_LOND">London</name>. I hope there has been no trouble. I shall write in a day or two. <seg xml:id="seg3" type="note-anchor">Weather</seg> continues very fine.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>Kindest regards</salute>                    	<signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
                        <dateline>
                            <date when="1922-10-16">16/x/922</date>
                        </dateline>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">The Joyces arrived at the Hotel Suisse and remained in Nice until mid-November 1922. In a letter to his <name type="person" key="murr62">Aunt Josephine</name> addressed from the Hotel Suisse at the end of October 1922, JJ references <name type="person" key="barn84">Nora</name>’s and <name type="person" key="joyc07">Lucia</name>’s company in Nice and anticipates that Giorgio, “who is in Paris will probably come down too as I think I shall take a place here for the winter” (<name type="work">JJ</name> 539). </note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">Writing four days after the publication of the Egoist Press edition of <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name>, JJ was probably awaiting press reports about its publication from London or Paris. JJ, however, would remain dissatisfied by the London and Paris press in the coming months (<name type="work">JJ</name> 541). </note>
                <note xml:id="note3" target="#seg3" type="commentary">Despite his initial intentions to travel to Nice, as its improved weather conditions might benefit his deteriorating eyes, weather eventually proved to be a problem. The rain and wind in Nice ultimately worsened JJ’s ocular problems, resulting in a consultation with Dr Louis Colin, who applied leeches and dionine solution to his eyes (<name type="work">JJ</name> 537-8).</note>
            </div>
      </body>
